{#
One cluster, as it appears in any view.

A single line: the calendar's colour, the time, then the title taking whatever
is left. Time first because in a dense grid that is the column you scan — the
title is what you read once you have found the row. `g:i a` matches how the mail
list writes times; the grid drops the meridiem to save the width and keeps it in
the tooltip.

**A chip with a height of its own puts a bar down its left, its time on the
first line beside it and its title under that.** One line of "10:00 Design review" in a 96px
column is four characters of each; two lines of title under a time is the whole
title and the time. Only where there IS a height to spend — a grid block sized
by its meeting's duration, a month cell with two rows to give — so the all-day
band, which is one line tall by definition, keeps everything on that line.

The stack is a grid with the parts PLACED rather than flowed, which is what lets
one source order serve all three arrangements: the agenda wants the time first,
the band wants the mark first, and the stack wants a bar beside both of them.
Flowing them would have meant a second copy of the markup for at least one.

**A bar rather than a dot, and the reason is the row next to it.** A month cell
holds all-day entries beside timed ones and an all-day entry has no time, so its
title starts on the first line where a timed one's starts on the second. With a
dot in front, those two titles begin 12px apart and every cell reads ragged down
its left edge. A bar is placed in a column of its own and spans whatever rows
there are, so the title starts at the same x whichever row it lands on — which
is also what the agenda does, for the same reason, two files apart.

The title takes the first FREE row of the content column, so a chip with no time
to print — an all-day entry, or a day a meeting merely runs through — puts it on
the first line and costs nothing for the line it does not need.

**Day view does not stack, and is told so rather than measured for it.** A day
column is the whole grid: a thousand pixels on a desktop page and still 264 in
the narrowest pane the handle allows, either of which holds a time and a title
on one line with room over. Stacked, that line's worth of text was drawn into
the top-left corner of a box a thousand pixels wide, with the title on a second
row under a "10:00" that had the other nine hundred to itself.

The caller's answer rather than a container query, because the caller knows it
for certain. A query would have to pick a width at which stacking stops paying,
and for a day column there isn't one: it is never the width that makes stacking
worth doing, whatever the window is up to.

**A month cell stacks as well, and it is the width that says so.** A month cell is
about 93px and a chip in one has 80. Inline, a dot and a time take 50 of those
and leave 30 for the title, which is four characters — and the first thing that
measured was a title unclamping to SIX lines inside the day's peek, because 30px
is not a width a title can finish in. Stacked, the same title has the whole 74.

So the difference between the month and the grid is not whether but how far. A
grid block has a duration's worth of height to spend and gives the title two
lines; a month cell has room for two chips of two lines each and gives it one,
which is 12 characters where inline gave four. The rest is the day's peek, where
the clamp comes off entirely — see _view_month.

**A month chip is clamped to one line of title and it STAYS clamped**, which is
what lets the day's peek widen without anything moving. This looks backwards
until you try it the other way: unclamping is what changes a chip's height, and
a chip changing height moves every chip under it. Clamped, a wider box is simply
a longer line — the same two rows, the same y for every neighbour, and a title
that finishes because there is now room for it to. The peek is the width; the
clamp is what makes the width free.

**The colour is a bar where the chip has a height, and a dot where it is one
line.** Same information, drawn as tall as there is room for it to be: a 6px
dot centred in a two-line agenda row or in an hour-tall grid block is a speck
with space around it, and the bar is the mark every other calendar draws. The
grid block ALSO carries the colour as a 10% wash over its whole area, which is
enough to group a week's meetings at a glance and not enough to tell two
similar calendars apart — and it cannot say the one thing a merged cluster has
to say, which is that this meeting is on two calendars at once. That is the
gradient below, and the bar carries it down its length where the dot carried it
around.

Accepts:
  cluster   OccurrenceCluster — the occurrences that draw this one chip
  showTime  whether to print the start time (default true; never for all-day,
            and never on a day the meeting only runs through — see below)
  tall      the chip fills a block that has a height of its own (default false)
  timeColumn  the time takes a lane of its own (default false)
  monthCell the chip is one row of a month cell (default false)
  tooltip   whether the chip carries its title as a hover tooltip (default:
            true, except in a month cell); off where hover already shows it
  stack     override for whether the chip stacks; Day view says false
  until     optional, printed at the end of a one-line chip — "until Sun 21:00"
            on the band's bar for an entry of a day or more, whose end is
            nowhere else on screen
  longTime  print the start on the full clock, meridiem and all (default
            false). The band's: a grid block's time is read against the axis
            beside it, and the band has no axis — "5:00" there is two times

`timeColumn` is the agenda's, and is the same kind of thing: a lane of its own
for the time, start over end, with "all day" written in it for a chip that has
no times to print. The lane's WIDTH is not this file's — the chip becomes a
`subgrid` row of the list it is in and the list's own track answers it, so the
lane is as wide as the widest time in the whole view. See _agenda_shell. Without it the title starts wherever the time happened to
end, and a list of rows does not line up down its left edge — which is the one
thing a list is for. It prints a label where the inline time prints nothing on
purpose: an all-day event and a day a meeting merely runs THROUGH both occupy
the whole of their day, so both are what "all day" means here, and a blank lane
would read as a missing value rather than as an answer.

`tall` is the time-grid's chip and is deliberately a set of classes rather than
a second markup block: the same mark, the same time, the same title, aligned to
the top of a box instead of centred in a line, with the title allowed to wrap
into the height the block already has instead of being truncated to one line.
Everything a chip IS stays one thing. It exists because the grid's blocks are
several lines tall and a chip centred in one of them reads as floating; it does
NOT exist to make a second kind of chip, which is why it changes no content and
adds no behaviour. The grid wraps this button in the positioned element that
carries the drag — the chip itself still only ever opens the editor.

**A chip whose words did not fit opens to them on hover.** The clipping is the
grid block's, not this file's — see `chip-open` in app.css — but the text inside
has to stop truncating at the same moment or the box would open onto an
ellipsis. Which is why the hover states below are written against `group/chip`
rather than against each span's own `:hover`: a title is uncovered by the
pointer being anywhere on the chip, including on the time beside it.

The two container-query steps are undone the same way, and their spelling is
deliberate. `@max-[4rem]:group-hover/chip:not-sr-only` is ONE rule with a
`:is()` in it, which outranks the plain `@max-[4rem]:sr-only` it has to beat; as
two separate classes the two would tie on specificity and the winner would be
whichever Tailwind happened to emit last. Which is a bug this codebase has
already had once, in `.hidden` against `.inline-block`.

**The chip gives up its words in a fixed order as its box narrows**, and it
asks the box rather than the viewport: `@container` is declared by the time
grid on each positioned block and on each all-day cell, so the question is
"how wide is THIS chip" — two overlapping meetings split a 96px column into
48px lanes, and no media query can see that. Under 7rem the time goes, because
the block's own position on the axis has been saying it all along and the title
has not. Under 4rem the title goes too and the chip is its colour and nothing
else. Both steps are `sr-only` rather than `hidden`: the text stays in the
accessibility tree and on the tooltip, which is the whole difference between
dropping a label and losing the information. Truncating instead is what this
used to do, and a chip reading "G…" spends the same pixels to say less.

A month cell declares one too, and its step is a different width and a bigger
drop. **Under 5.5rem a month chip gives up its words entirely** and becomes what
it has left to say — a colour and a position: one full-width bar per entry,
stacked down the day, which is what every calendar on a phone draws in a month
cell.

5.5rem rather than the 4 the grid uses, because a month cell spends its width
differently. A grid block at 4rem is a lane of a column that still has an hour
line and a position to say when it is; a month cell has neither, so its chip
has to carry a whole time AND a title or it is carrying an ellipsis. Seven
columns of a 768px window with the sidebar out are 74px each, and 74px printed
`E2E…` under `14:00` — text present, nothing said. The same number as the peek's
gate above it, and deliberately: below it there is nothing a wider box would
reveal, which is exactly when a peek is not worth opening.

An agenda row declares no container and is unaffected — a size query with no
container to ask evaluates false, which is the right default and not a
coincidence to rely on quietly.

**A cluster of one is the ordinary case and renders exactly as it always did.**
The views hand over clusters rather than occurrences so there is one code path
for them, not because most chips are merged — almost none are. Everything below
normalises onto `occurrence` and `event`, which is what the markup reads.

A cluster of several is one meeting that legitimately exists twice: extracted
from its invitation onto the account's calendar, and mirrored from the provider
onto a connected one, both carrying the organiser's UID. Its dot is a
conic-gradient of the member calendars' colours in equal slices — the same
1.5×1.5 dot, so the row's rhythm does not change — and the tooltip names the
calendars, because a two-tone dot says "more than one" and nothing else. The
slice arithmetic is done here rather than on the DTO: it is a CSS value, and
the DTO carries the colours a chip may draw with, not the way it draws them.

`text-left` is not decoration. This is a <button>, and a button centres its text
by default, which is what had every chip in the month grid sitting oddly
mid-cell.

Opens the editor in the body-level modal frame — never inside the calendar
pane, which carries backdrop-filter and would clip a fixed-position dialog to
itself. The controller has to be on the trigger: a Stimulus action only reaches
controllers mounted on an ancestor, and the modal's lives on #modal-backdrop.

**This is the only place an editor is opened on an occurrence**, and it is why
the three views include this partial rather than each writing their own button.
The URL carries `instance` — the occurrence's recurrence id, which is where the
rule ORIGINALLY put it, not where an override may since have moved it — because
without it the editor knows the event and not which of its occurrences was
clicked, and "this event" has nothing to key a patch on. UTC, and read back by
EventInstanceEditor::instance(), which owns both directions of that spelling.

The URL names the PRIMARY member's event, and only that one. The editor
re-derives the rest of the cluster from that event's uid, so no cluster
identity is threaded through a URL that would outlive the render that made it.
#}

{% set occurrence = cluster.primary %}
{% set event = occurrence.event %}
{# `?? true`, not `|default(true)`: the default filter treats false as absent,
   so the time-grid's `showTime: false` never took and every chip printed a start
   time the column's own axis was already showing. #}
{% set showTime = (showTime ?? true) and not event.isAllDay %}
{% set tall = tall|default(false) %}
{% set timeColumn = timeColumn|default(false) %}

{# Whether the chip has a height of its own to fill, which is the one question
   the mark's shape and the hover growth both turn on. The two ways of having
   one are a grid block, which is as tall as its meeting lasts, and an agenda
   row, which is as tall as its two lines of time. #}
{% set monthCell = monthCell|default(false) %}
{# `??` for the reason `stack` gives below: callers pass false. #}
{% set tooltip = tooltip ?? not monthCell %}
{% set roomy = timeColumn or tall or monthCell %}

{# The month's. A month cell is the one place a title had no way to finish —
   `tall` needs a block with a height and `timeColumn` needs a list with rows,
   and a cell has neither — so it is one line, and stays one line on hover.
   Unclamping the chip under the pointer pushed every chip below it down, which
   is movement where the reader asked for a larger view. Hovering the day widens
   the whole cell instead (see _month_shell's `shellCellExpands`). #}

{# Whether the chip prints "18:00–19:00" or just "18:00".

   Off by default and switched on by the agenda alone, which is the one view
   with room for it and the one that needs it: a grid block already SHOWS its
   length by being that long, and a month cell is too narrow to spend eleven
   characters on a time. An agenda row draws no timeline at all, so a start on
   its own leaves "how long is this?" unanswerable without opening the event.

   Never for an all-day chip, which has no times worth printing — that is
   already what showTime decides, and this rides on it rather than repeating the
   condition. The en dash and the absence of spaces around it match the ranges
   in _invite_card, _proposal_card and sharing/_entry_row; there is one way this
   application writes a span of time. #}
{% set showRange = showTime and showRange|default(false) %}

{# The clock this chip's time is printed on, and it is the VIEW's, not the
   reader's default.
 
   A time-grid block is positioned against the calendar's zone — see
   DayGridLayout, and CalendarRangeReader::zoneOf(), which is where that zone
   comes from — while Twig's `|date` converts to whatever TwigTimezoneSubscriber
   set, which is the user's own. Where the two differ, and they do for every
   calendar provisioned before the zone was seeded from the user, the block was
   drawn on the 15:00 line with "17:30" written inside it. The grid was right
   and the label was on a different clock, which is the one failure mode of that
   view the file's own header warns about.
 
   Falls back to `null` — the reader's clock — for a caller that has no view
   zone to offer, which is every context outside the calendar grids. #}
{% set chip_zone = zone|default(range.zone|default(null)) %}

{# A day this meeting merely RUNS THROUGH, rather than the day it begins.

   CalendarRangeReader deliberately places a multi-day event on every day it
   touches — otherwise it vanishes from the week whose Monday it began before —
   and until now every one of those days printed the same start time. A Friday
   15:00 event ending on Sunday therefore drew "15:00" three times, which reads
   as three one-hour events rather than as one that lasts the weekend, and that
   is exactly how it has been reported.

   The start time is simply not a fact about a continuation day: the meeting
   does not start at 15:00 on Saturday, it is already running. So the time is
   left off and the title stands alone, which is what an event occupying the
   whole of a day looks like everywhere else on this screen.

   `dayKey` is the day the caller is drawing and it is ambient rather than
   passed: the month and agenda shells both set it, and a caller that has none
   — Happening Soon, a share page's list — falls back to null and nothing here
   changes. Compared on the chip's own clock, never the reader's default, for
   the reason chip_zone exists at all. #}
{% set chip_day = dayKey|default(null) %}
{% set showTime = showTime and (chip_day is null
    or occurrence.startsAt|date('Y-m-d', chip_zone) == chip_day) %}

{# Set here rather than up with `tall`, because it turns on `showTime` and
   `showTime` is not final until the continuation-day rule above has had its
   say. #}
{# `??`, not `|default`, for the reason showTime gives above: the caller that
   passes this passes FALSE, and the default filter treats false as absent. #}
{% set stacked = stack ?? (tall or monthCell) %}

{% set until = until|default(null) %}
{% set longTime = longTime|default(false) %}

{% set chip_title = event.title ~ (showTime
    ? ' · ' ~ occurrence.startsAt|date(clock.time, chip_zone)
        ~ (showRange ? '–' ~ occurrence.endsAt|date(clock.time, chip_zone) : '')
    : '') ~ (until is not null ? ' · ' ~ until : '') %}

{% if cluster.isMerged %}
    {% set chip_title = chip_title ~ ' · ' ~ 'calendar.chip.on_calendars'|trans({
        '%calendars%': cluster.calendarNames|join(', '),
    }) %}

    {% set slices = [] %}
    {% for color in cluster.colors %}
        {% set slices = slices|merge([
            color
                ~ ' ' ~ (loop.index0 * 100 / cluster.colors|length)|round(3) ~ '%'
                ~ ' ' ~ (loop.index * 100 / cluster.colors|length)|round(3) ~ '%',
        ]) %}
    {% endfor %}

    {# The same stops read two ways: around a dot, down a bar. `slices` is a
       list of `colour from% to%` triples, which is the one syntax both gradient
       functions take, so the shape is the only thing that changes here. #}
    {% set mark_style = 'background: '
        ~ (roomy ? 'linear-gradient(to bottom, ' : 'conic-gradient(')
        ~ slices|join(', ') ~ ')' %}
{% else %}
    {% set mark_style = 'background-color: ' ~ event.calendar.color %}
{% endif %}

<button type="button"
   data-controller="ui--modal"
   data-action="click->ui--modal#open"
   {# Details, not the editor. Clicking an event to find out when it is used
      to open a form asking you to change it — and the three things people
      most often want (the description, who else is coming, and which mail it
      came from) are not on that form at all. Edit is one step further in. #}
   data-ui--modal-src-value="{{ path('app_calendar_event_details', {
       id: event.id,
       instance: occurrence.recurrenceId|date('Y-m-d\\TH:i:s\\Z', 'UTC'),
   }) }}"
   {# NOT calendar.event.title — that is the label on the title FIELD in the
      editor, and reusing it here headed the details dialog with the word
      "Title" above a card that shows the event's name, its dates and its
      calendar. A heading naming one field of a form is wrong over a summary of
      the whole event. #}
   data-ui--modal-title-value="{{ 'calendar.event.details_heading'|trans }}"
   {# No tooltip where hovering already shows the whole title: the month
      cell enlarges, a time-grid block or all-day chip opens to its content,
      and an agenda row unwraps its title. There a tooltip only repeats what is on screen, and a second
      box opening under the pointer is flicker. The string stays the chip's
      accessible name: it carries the merged calendars, which are not printed. #}
   {% if tooltip %}title="{{ chip_title }}"{% else %}aria-label="{{ chip_title }}"{% endif %}
   class="group/chip w-full text-left rounded-md
          hover:bg-hover transition-colors min-w-0
          {# `items-start` on the stack, not `items-center`: they agree
             whenever the words fit, and part company in a block too short for
             them — a half-hour meeting in a 96px column — where the rows shrink
             below their text and a centred title spilled UP over the time
             above it. Top-aligned, it overflows downwards into the clip. #}
          {{ timeColumn
              ? 'col-span-full grid grid-cols-subgrid py-1 border-t border-line/60 first:border-t-0'
              : (stacked
                  ? 'grid grid-cols-[max-content_minmax(0,1fr)] gap-x-1.5 content-start items-start px-1.5 py-0.5'
                    ~ (monthCell ? ' @max-[5.5rem]:block @max-[5.5rem]:px-0 @max-[5.5rem]:py-0' : '')
                  : 'flex gap-1.5 px-1.5 py-0.5') }}
          {# Inset: on the time grid this button fills a block that is
             `rounded-md overflow-hidden`, so a ring outside the button was
             outside the block and clipped away in full — focus with no focus
             ring at all. See mail/_attachment_chip.html.twig. #}
          focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-inset focus-visible:ring-accent
          @max-[4rem]:justify-center
          {{ tall
              ? 'h-full overflow-hidden ' ~ (stacked ? '' : 'items-start')
              : (timeColumn or stacked ? 'items-start' : 'items-center') }}
          {{ occurrence.cancelled ? 'opacity-50' : '' }}"
>
    {% if timeColumn %}
        {# No width here. The lane is a track of the list's grid and is sized
           against every time in it — which is what stops a range needing eleven
           characters on one line, and what lets a translated "all day" have the
           width it actually wants instead of wrapping inside a guess. #}
        <span class="text-[11px] leading-4 tabular-nums text-ink-faint">
            {%- if showTime -%}
                <span class="block text-ink-muted">
                    {{- occurrence.startsAt|date(clock.timeCompact, chip_zone) -}}
                </span>
                {%- if showRange -%}
                    <span class="block">
                        {{- occurrence.endsAt|date(clock.timeCompact, chip_zone) -}}
                    </span>
                {%- endif -%}
            {%- else -%}
                {{- 'calendar.grid.all_day'|trans -}}
            {%- endif -%}
        </span>
    {% elseif showTime %}
        {# The width step is for the single-line shape only. A stacked chip has
           given the time a line of its own, so there is nothing for it to be
           competing with and nothing to give up. #}
        <span class="shrink-0 text-[11px] tabular-nums text-ink-faint
                     {{ stacked
                         ? 'col-start-2 row-start-1 leading-4' ~ (monthCell ? ' @max-[5.5rem]:sr-only' : '')
                         : '@max-[7rem]:sr-only @max-[7rem]:group-hover/chip:not-sr-only' }}">
            {{- occurrence.startsAt|date(longTime ? clock.time : clock.timeCompact, chip_zone) -}}
            {%- if showRange -%}
                –{{ occurrence.endsAt|date(clock.timeCompact, chip_zone) }}
            {%- endif -%}
        </span>
    {% endif %}

    {# The calendar's colour. Written after the time in SOURCE and pulled back
       in front of it with `order-first` everywhere except the agenda, which is
       the one view that wants the time at the left edge — a list is read down
       its first column, and that column should be the thing that differs from
       row to row rather than a mark that is usually the same all the way down.
       Reordering rather than printing it twice, and safe to reorder because
       `aria-hidden` means it has no place in the reading order to disturb. #}
    <span class="shrink-0 rounded-full
                 {{ roomy ? 'w-1 self-stretch' : 'w-1.5 h-1.5' }}
                 {# `block` with them, and it is not spare. This span is a grid
                    item in every other shape the chip takes, which blockifies
                    it; with the button collapsed to `display: block` it is an
                    inline span again, and an inline span ignores a width and a
                    height both. The bar rendered as nothing at all. #}
                 {{ monthCell ? '@max-[5.5rem]:block @max-[5.5rem]:w-full @max-[5.5rem]:h-1.5' : '' }}
                 {{ stacked
                     ? 'col-start-1 row-span-full'
                     : (timeColumn ? '' : 'order-first') }}"
          style="{{ mark_style }}"
          aria-hidden="true"></span>

    {# Truncated to one line in a list, wrapped into the height it has in a
       grid block. `truncate` is `overflow:hidden` plus `white-space:nowrap`, so
       leaving it on a tall block would waste every line but the first. #}
    <span class="min-w-0 flex-1 text-xs text-ink
                 {{ stacked ? 'col-start-2' : '' }}
                 {{ monthCell
                     ? '@max-[5.5rem]:sr-only'
                     : '@max-[4rem]:sr-only @max-[4rem]:group-hover/chip:not-sr-only' }}
                 {{ tall
                     ? 'whitespace-normal break-words line-clamp-2 leading-4 group-hover/chip:line-clamp-none'
                     : (monthCell
                         ? 'whitespace-normal break-words line-clamp-1 leading-4'
                         : 'truncate') }}
                 {{ roomy and not tall ? 'group-hover/chip:whitespace-normal' : '' }}
                 {{ occurrence.cancelled ? 'line-through' : '' }}">
        {%- if event.kind is not null -%}
            <i class="{{ event.kind.icon }} mr-1 text-[10px] text-ink-faint" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        {%- endif -%}
        {#- The badge a booked event carries, and the only source that has one.
            EventSource::icon() answers null for every other case, so this
            renders nothing on an ordinary chip and cannot be forgotten for a
            future source — the match there has no default.

            Accented rather than faint, unlike the extraction icon above it: an
            hour a stranger put in the calendar has to be distinguishable at a
            glance from one the owner typed, which is the requirement, and
            another grey mark beside a grey mark is not. Both can appear
            together in principle and never do in practice — a booked event
            carries no ExtractionKind, by the argument in EventSource::Booking. -#}
        {%- if event.source.icon is not null -%}
            <i class="{{ event.source.icon }} mr-1 text-[10px] text-accent"
               title="{{ event.source.badgeTransKey|trans }}"
               aria-label="{{ event.source.badgeTransKey|trans }}"></i>
        {%- endif -%}
        {{ event.title }}
    </span>

    {# After the title, which truncates first: the end is the part a bar
       across several days cannot say any other way. #}
    {% if until is not null %}
        <span class="shrink-0 text-[11px] tabular-nums text-ink-faint whitespace-nowrap">{{ until }}</span>
    {% endif %}
</button>
